  "summary": "Bucky O’Hare is Konami’s NES platform action game based on the comic-book space hero. The rabbit captain’s crew has been captured and scattered across four planets, leaving Bucky to begin the rescue alone. Those first destinations can be approached in different orders. Each rescued companion then becomes playable, bringing another weapon or ability to the team. The result turns the rescue story into a changing set of controls: progress adds people whose skills can alter how the next obstacle is crossed.",
  "body": [
    "Bucky O’Hare is Konami’s NES platform action game based on the comic-book space hero. The rabbit captain’s crew has been captured and scattered across four planets, leaving Bucky to begin the rescue alone. Those first destinations can be approached in different orders. Each rescued companion then becomes playable, bringing another weapon or ability to the team. The result turns the rescue story into a changing set of controls: progress adds people whose skills can alter how the next obstacle is crossed.",
    "The opening worlds give the journey distinct physical challenges. The Green Planet combines trees and waterfalls, the Red Planet is dominated by volcanic heat, the Blue Planet is icy, and the Yellow Planet contains cannons and moving asteroids. Those settings ask for careful movement as well as shooting. The player can switch between available characters during play, so rescuing a companion expands the answers available to a difficult section. The order of rescue becomes a way of shaping which abilities arrive first.",
    "Once the initial crew is reunited, the adventure moves into another captivity aboard the Magma Tanker. The company must continue saving one another and work toward escape, giving the second part a different structure from the opening choice of planets. The game combines colourful character animation with short, demanding platform sections, encouraging repeated attempts at a particular movement. Its team structure supplies continuity through those changes: the characters are both the reason for the journey and the tools with which the player completes it."
  ],
  "story_headings": [
    "Bring the crew home",
    "A planet for each problem",
    "A rescue that keeps changing hands"
  ],
